# Integration test: boot MkLinux DR3 on every PowerPC machine we model.
#
# One published image — `systems/mklinux_dr3_76_single_169mb.img`, 169 MB —
# carrying Mac OS 7.6 *and* MkLinux DR3 on one Apple partition map:
#
#   1  Apple            Apple_partition_map        63 @ 1
#   2  Macintosh        Apple_Driver43             54 @ 64
#   3  Macintosh        Apple_Driver43             74 @ 118
#   4  Patch Partition  Apple_Patches             512 @ 192
#   5  untitled         Apple_HFS               81920 @ 704       (40.0M)  Mac OS 7.6
#   6  root             Apple_UNIX_SVR2        196608 @ 82624     (96.0M)  ext2, /
#   7  swap             Apple_UNIX_SVR2         32768 @ 279232    (16.0M)
#   8  Extra            Apple_Free              34230 @ 312000    (16.7M)
#
# Mac OS is the boot path on this hardware generation — MkLinux never boots the
# machine itself.  The MkLinux Booter extension in the System Folder on
# partition 5 puts up a splash, and its ten-second timeout (installed default
# `bootos=MkLinux`) loads `Mach Kernel` and hands over.  Nobody clicks
# anything.
#
# ONE IMAGE, FOUR MACHINES.  The console DR3 prints is identical on the three
# NuBus models — same kernel, same userland, nothing machine-specific on the
# disk — so the same `mklinux-login.png` is the success criterion on all of
# them.  That is the strongest statement this suite can make about the image
# being machine-independent, and the reason it is worth running on every commit
# rather than nightly.
#
# The 7500 is the fourth machine and the reason the image carries 7.6 rather
# than 7.5: a PCI Power Mac refuses a 7.5 startup volume with DSErrCode $66
# (dsOldSystem) before the Booter is ever reached.  It runs the same disk and
# the same kernel, and is asserted WITHOUT a golden — see row_pm7500().
#
# WAIT FOR THE SCREEN, DO NOT BUDGET FOR IT.  Every row uses wait_match, which
# returns on the first quantum whose frame equals the golden.  A fixed budget
# would pay for the slowest machine on every row and then keep running after
# the screen it wanted was already up; the ceilings below are hang detectors,
# not runtimes.  (wait_stable is the wrong tool here: the Booter's splash
# counts down in seconds and the Linux console blinks its cursor, so nothing
# on screen ever holds still.)
#
# RAM IS PER MODEL, and it is not cosmetic.  Each machine gets its own
# ram_default: 24 MB on the 6100 and 7100, 40 MB on the 8100 (whose ladder has
# no 24 MB rung at all — asking for one is a hard error, not a rounding).  At
# 40 MB the 6100 never brings up 8-bit video and MkLinux's console is
# invisible, which reads exactly like a boot failure and is not one.

include "../lib/mac.script"

# In gs-test-data since 9e807e1 — `systems/mklinux_dr3_76_single_169mb.img.7z`,
# extracted in place by fetch-test-data.sh.  No have_media() guard: the data is
# part of the pinned corpus, so a missing image is a failure to report.
let disk = "../../data/systems/mklinux_dr3_76_single_169mb.img"

# The 7500 needs its own ROM, so it cannot come through TEST_ROM/$ROM.  Same
# fixture tnt-hd-boot uses: the 1995-08 TNT ROM v1.
let tnt_rom = "../../data/roms/pm7500-pm8500-pm9500-96cd923d.rom"

# Power on with the disk attached and run to the point where the Booter's
# splash is up.  The RTC is seeded AFTER machine.boot (which reseeds it from
# the host clock) and later than the install's base, so the installed files are
# in the past.
def power_on(model, ram_kb) {
    machine.boot model="${$model}" ram=$ram_kb rom="${$ROM}"
    machine.rtc.time = 0xE1200000
    machine.scsi.attach_hd "${$disk}" 0
    # suite-pdm's boot_pdm_75() head: chip init and bus scan, then the
    # documented Ticks poke past the 20 s drive-spin-up window.
    scheduler.run 360000000
    machine.memory.poke.l 0x9716A 0x2000
}

# Same, for the PCI machine: its own ROM, and none of the PDM spin-up dance —
# the Ticks poke above pokes a Mac OS low-memory global past the NuBus SCSI
# scan's wait, which the TNT boot path does not perform.  (tnt-hd-boot does not
# poke either.)
def power_on_tnt(model, ram_kb) {
    machine.boot model="${$model}" ram=$ram_kb rom="${$tnt_rom}"
    machine.rtc.time = 0xE1200000
    machine.scsi.attach_hd "${$disk}" 0
}

# Wait for a golden WITHOUT ever writing it.  mac.script's wait_match captures
# under REGEN=1, which is right for the row that owns a golden and wrong for
# every other row: a row that is failing would quietly record its own failure
# as the reference.  Only the pm7100 row owns the login golden; the rest wait
# on it with this, which cannot write anything.  (Row order matters for the
# same reason — pm7100 runs first so the golden exists.)
def wait_for(golden, ceiling) {
    let spent = 0
    while $spent < $ceiling {
        scheduler.run 20000000
        $spent = $spent + 20000000
        if machine.screen.matches("${$golden}") { return $spent }
    }
    machine.screen.match "${$golden}"
    return $spent
}

# MkLinux reached a shell AND its own filesystem is under it.
#
# Run on the pm7100 row only, and that is enough for all three: every row is
# matched against the SAME golden, so a golden that is secretly a mid-boot
# frame (which REGEN has produced twice now) fails here and takes the whole
# suite with it.  Paying for it on every row would cost the login typing and
# two 169 MB exports three times over for no extra coverage.
#
# The baseline is taken at the login prompt, not before the boot: Mac OS mounts
# the HFS partition on the way through and writes its volume metadata, so a
# comparison against the published image is satisfied by Mac OS alone — which
# is exactly the false green this row reported the first time it was written.
# Between the login prompt and this `sync` nothing but MkLinux is running, so
# any difference at all is MkLinux writing to /dev/sda6.
def prove_root_is_mounted(model) {
    machine.scsi.device[0].image.export "${$WORK_DIR}/${$model}-at-login.img"
    machine.adb.keyboard.type "root\n"
    scheduler.run 250000000
    machine.adb.keyboard.type "mklinux\n"
    scheduler.run 800000000
    machine.adb.keyboard.type "sync\n"
    scheduler.run 700000000
    machine.scsi.device[0].image.export "${$WORK_DIR}/${$model}-after-sync.img"
    let moved = storage.path_compare("${$WORK_DIR}/${$model}-at-login.img", "${$WORK_DIR}/${$model}-after-sync.img")
    echo "${$model}: the disk moved at offset ${$moved} between the login prompt and sync"
    assert $moved != -1 "${$model}: logging in and syncing wrote nothing — no root filesystem is mounted"
}

# Ride the splash timeout to the login prompt and stop the moment it appears.
# Measured arrivals: 0.3 G on the 7100, 1.4 G on the 6100, 2.1 G on the 8100.
# 4 G is a hang detector with room, not a runtime — every row stops the moment
# the prompt is on screen.
def ride_to_login(model, ram_kb) {
    power_on("${$model}", $ram_kb)
    let spent = wait_for("mklinux-login.png", 4000000000)
    echo "${$model}: login prompt after ${$spent} instructions"
    # Structural corroboration, so a failure says WHERE it stopped rather than
    # only that a golden did not match: the 68k address space is gone.  A 7.5
    # boot answers low memory; a machine running the Mach kernel does not.
    assert try(machine.cpu.mmu.peek(0x17B, 1), none) == none "${$model}: still in the 68k address space — the Booter never handed off"
    assert machine.screen.depth == 8 "${$model}: ${machine.screen.depth} bpp, want 8 — MkLinux's console needs the 256-colour mode"
}

# The 7100 row carries the extras, so the suite pays for three boots and not
# five: the Booter's splash (which is Mac OS running, and therefore the control
# that separates "MkLinux broke" from "the disk broke"), and a real session.
def row_pm7100() {
    power_on("pm7100", 24576)
    # `Root Device: /dev/sda6` is the install's lilo.conf edit, read back by
    # the software that consumes it.  The countdown line is masked — it is a
    # live clock — which is why this one is a match-with-mask and not a
    # wait_match.
    scheduler.run 450000000
    check_ex("booter-splash.png", 300, 420, 340, 570)

    # This row OWNS the login golden, and it captures it on a FIXED budget
    # rather than with wait_match.  wait_match under REGEN=1 stops as soon as
    # the screen holds still for three polls — and the Mach kernel's early
    # console holds perfectly still while the SCSI probe runs, so it captures
    # `asc1: NCR 53C94 20 mhz SCSI ID 7` and calls that the login screen.  Both
    # other rows then "match" it and the suite goes green on a mid-boot frame.
    # (Measured: that is exactly what happened.)  This is a fixed budget with
    # room, ~19 s of wall clock, and the only row that pays it.
    #
    # It went from 1.5 G to 2.4 G when the CD bay joined the bus (system_create
    # now carries the drive from power-on, empty or not): Linux probes the
    # empty drive and waits out its NOT READY, which moved the prompt's
    # arrival from well inside the old budget to a measured 2.61 G total.
    scheduler.run 2400000000
    check("mklinux-login.png")
    assert try(machine.cpu.mmu.peek(0x17B, 1), none) == none "pm7100: still in the 68k address space"
    assert machine.screen.depth == 8 "pm7100: ${machine.screen.depth} bpp, want 8"
    prove_root_is_mounted("pm7100")
    report_row("DR3", "ariel", "mklinux-boot single-disk login")
    report_perf("mklinux-boot/pm7100", scheduler.instr_count)
}

def row_pm6100() { ride_to_login("pm6100", 24576) }
def row_pm8100() { ride_to_login("pm8100", 40960) }

# The PCI machine, and the row the 7.6 image exists for.
#
# NO GOLDEN, DELIBERATELY.  The 7500 comes up in 32-bit colour and MkLinux's
# console is drawn very dark there — the text is legible and identical in
# content to the other three, but nothing like `mklinux-login.png`, so this row
# cannot share it.  Nor does it get one of its own: we believe that rendering
# is a Control-video palette bug, and a golden of it would enshrine the bug as
# the expected result and go red on the day it is fixed.  Until that is
# understood, this row asserts what is not in doubt — the machine left the 68k
# address space, and the root filesystem takes writes.
#
# It therefore runs a FIXED budget instead of waiting on a frame, which is
# safe here for two reasons.  The emulator is deterministic, so a measured
# figure holds; and a budget that is too short cannot produce a false green —
# the login typing lands nowhere, nothing is written, and prove_root_is_mounted
# fails loudly.  Measured on this image: the 68k address space is still
# answering at 0.5 G and gone by 1.0 G, and the console reaches its final state
# by 2.0 G (screen checksum identical at 2.0, 2.5 and 3.0 G).  3 G is the
# margin on that.
#
# Do NOT convert this back into a poll on the address-space check.  Written
# that way it broke on the first iteration and reported a hand-off 20 M
# instructions in, which is an order of magnitude too early to be real: the
# check is only meaningful once Mac OS has had a chance to set low memory up,
# so as a loop condition it fires on the pre-boot state instead.  As an
# assertion AFTER the run it says exactly what it should.
def row_pm7500() {
    power_on_tnt("pm7500", 32768)
    scheduler.run 3000000000
    assert try(machine.cpu.mmu.peek(0x17B, 1), none) == none "pm7500: still in the 68k address space — the Booter never handed off"
    assert machine.screen.depth == 32 "pm7500: ${machine.screen.depth} bpp, want 32"
    prove_root_is_mounted("pm7500")
    report_row("DR3", "control", "mklinux-boot single-disk login")
    report_perf("mklinux-boot/pm7500", scheduler.instr_count)
}

# pm7100 first: it owns the login golden the other NuBus rows wait on.
if row_on("pm7100") { row_end("pm7100", try(row_pm7100(), "FAILED")) }
if row_on("pm6100") { row_end("pm6100", try(row_pm6100(), "FAILED")) }
if row_on("pm8100") { row_end("pm8100", try(row_pm8100(), "FAILED")) }
if row_on("pm7500") { row_end("pm7500", try(row_pm7500(), "FAILED")) }
suite_done()

quit
